{:edb/db-before-t 1617 :edb/db-after-t 1618 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534930 50 #inst "2026-10-08T11:17:15.850Z" 13194139534930 true] [17592186147297 1100 "Find the Laplace transform of $f'(t)$ for $s > 3$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous over $[0,\\infty),$ $f(0)=0,$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} =  \\dfrac{6}{s^2-5s+6}, \\quad s>3.\n$$\n\nAssume that $f'$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty).$" 13194139534930 true] [17592186147297 1100 "Find the Laplace transform of $f'(t)$ for $s > 3$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous over $[MATH: [0,\\infty),]$ $f(0)=0$, and\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = \\dfrac{6}{s^2-5s+6}, \\quad s>3$.\nAssume that $f'$ is piecewise continuous on $[MATH: [0,\\infty).]$" 13194139534930 false] [17592186147297 1145 "Suppose\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[MATH: [0,\\infty).]$ If $f'(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[MATH: [0,\\infty),]$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f'(t) \\right\\} = s \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t) \\right\\} - f(0)\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of $f'(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f^{′}(t)} &= sL{f(t)} - f(0) \\\\\n&= s \\cdot \\frac{6}{s^{2} - 5s + 6} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{6s}{s^{2} - 5s + 6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s > 3$." 13194139534930 false] [17592186147297 1145 "Suppose $F(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\}$ where $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[0,\\infty).$ If $f'(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty),$ then\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{ f'(t) \\right\\}  = s \\mathcal{L}\\left\\{  f(t)  \\right\\} -  f(0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of $f'(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{′}(t)} & =sL{f(t)}-f(0) \\\\  & =s\\cdot \\frac{6}{{s}^{2}-5s+6}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{6s}{{s}^{2}-5s+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s > 3.$" 13194139534930 true] [17592186147298 1100 "Find the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f''(t) \\right\\}$ for $s > 0$, given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous on $[MATH: [0,\\infty),]$ $f'(0)=2$, $f(0)=-1$, and\n$\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} = \\dfrac{2 - s}{s^2+4}, \\quad s>0$.\nAssume that $f'$ is continuous and $f''$ piecewise continuous on $[MATH: [0,\\infty).]$" 13194139534930 false] [17592186147298 1100 "Find the Laplace transform $\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f''(t) \\right\\}$ for $s > 0,$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous on $[0,\\infty),$ $f'(0)=2,$ $f(0)=-1,$ and\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{L}\\left\\{f(t)\\right\\} =   \\dfrac{2 - s}{s^2+4}, \\quad s>0.\n$$\n\nAssume that $f'$ is continuous and $f''$ piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty).$" 13194139534930 true] [17592186147298 1145 "Suppose\n\n$$\nF(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[MATH: [0,\\infty).]$ If $f'(t)$ is continuous and $f''(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[MATH: [0,\\infty),]$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f^{″}(t)} &= s^{2}L{f(t)} - sf(0) - f^{′}(0)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of $f''(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL{f^{″}(t)} &= s^{2}L{f(t)} - sf(0) - f^{′}(0) \\\\\n&= s^{2} \\cdot \\frac{2 - s}{s^{2} + 4} - s \\cdot (-1) - 2 \\\\\n&= (s^{2}(2 - s))/(s^{2} + 4) + s - 2 \\\\\n&= - (s^{2}(s - 2))/(s^{2} + 4) + (s - 2) \\\\\n&= (s - 2)(-\\frac{s^{2}}{s^{2} + 4} + 1) \\\\\n&= (s - 2)(\\frac{4}{s^{2} + 4}) \\\\\n&= (4(s - 2))/(s^{2} + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s > 0$." 13194139534930 false] [17592186147298 1145 "Suppose $F(s) = \\mathcal L \\{f(t)\\}$ where $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[0,\\infty).$ If $f'(t)$ is continuous and $f''(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{″}(t)} & ={s}^{2}L{f(t)}-sf(0)-{f}^{′}(0).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of $f''(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{″}(t)} & ={s}^{2}L{f(t)}-sf(0)-{f}^{′}(0) \\\\  & ={s}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{2-s}{{s}^{2}+4}-s\\cdot (-1)-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{{s}^{2}(2-s)}{{s}^{2}+4}+s-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{{s}^{2}(s-2)}{{s}^{2}+4}+(s-2) \\\\  & =(s-2)(-\\frac{{s}^{2}}{{s}^{2}+4}+1) \\\\  & =(s-2)(\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4(s-2)}{{s}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nvalid for $s > 0.$" 13194139534930 true] [17592186147447 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320531 13194139534930 true] [17592186147447 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320538 13194139534930 true] [17592186147447 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320552 13194139534930 true] [17592186147447 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320566 13194139534930 true] [17592186147448 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320545 13194139534930 true] [17592186147448 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320559 13194139534930 true] [17592186147448 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320573 13194139534930 true] [17592186147448 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320580 13194139534930 true] [17592186147448 1020 #edb/ref 17592186320587 13194139534930 true] [17592186148091 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534930 true] [17592186148091 1100 "Find the Laplace transform of $f^{′}(t)$ for $s > 3$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous over $[MATH: [0, ∞),]$ $f(0) = 2$, and\n\n$L{f(t)} = \\frac{2s - 1}{s^{2} - s - 6}, s > 3$.\nAssume that $f^{′}$ is piecewise continuous on $[MATH: [0, ∞).]$" 13194139534930 false] [17592186148091 1100 "Find the Laplace transform of ${f}^{′}(t)$ for $s>3$ given that the function $f(t)$ is continuous over $[0,\\infty ),$ $f(0)=2,$ and\n\n$$\nL{f(t)}=\\frac{2s-1}{{s}^{2}-s-6},\\,s>3.\n$$\n\nAssume that ${f}^{′}$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ).$" 13194139534930 true] [17592186148091 1145 "Suppose $F(s)=L{f(t)}$ where $f(t)$ is a continuous function of exponential order $a$ on $[0,\\infty ).$ If ${f}^{′}(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty ),$ then\n\n$$\nL{{f}^{′}(t)}=sL{f(t)}-f(0).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the Laplace transform of ${f}^{′}(t)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{f}^{′}(t)} & =sL{f(t)}-f(0) \\\\  